**New Evidence**
- Biden’s 2022 economic frame was explicit: he said the administration was pursuing a bottom-up, middle-out economy and would tackle higher costs and the post-pandemic recovery as a governing project.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"FACT SHEET: Background on President Biden's Remarks on the Economy During His First State of the Union Address","publisher":"The American Presidency Project","publication_date":"2022-02-28","url":"https://www.presidency.ucsb.edu/documents/fact-sheet-background-president-bidens-remarks-the-economy-during-his-first-state-the","claim":"The White House said Biden's 2022 State of the Union economic frame was to grow the economy from the bottom up and the middle out, and that the first year had produced record job growth and a faster recovery than other advanced economies.","locator":"lines 62-65","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct White House fact sheet tied to the president's address; explicit and contemporaneous, though it is self-description.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within 2022 program articulation.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to Biden and his administration's stated program.","contrary_evidence":["It states goals and claimed achievements rather than independently proving execution."],"lenses":["8B.1","8B.7","8B.10"]}

- The 2022 Economic Report transmittal says Biden intended to repair pandemic damage, pivot away from an economy that rewards wealth over work, and invest in people, innovation, infrastructure, and climate.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Message to the Congress Transmitting the Economic Report of the President","publisher":"The American Presidency Project","publication_date":"2022-04-14","url":"https://www.presidency.ucsb.edu/documents/message-the-congress-transmitting-the-economic-report-the-president","claim":"Biden said he came into office to repair pandemic harms, turn the page on an economy that rewards wealth over work, and lay the groundwork for a bottom-up, middle-out economy through investments in people, innovation, infrastructure, and climate.","locator":"lines 95-106","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct presidential transmittal of the economic report with clear strategic language.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"2022","ruler_attribution":"Direct presidential statement and priority-setting.","contrary_evidence":["Program statement only; not proof of implementation by itself."],"lenses":["8B.1","8B.2","8B.10"]}

- Biden’s September cabinet meeting remarks give direct evidence of implementation planning: he named the infrastructure law, CHIPS, IRA, student debt relief, and ARP as the agenda and told agencies to produce plans.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Remarks Prior to a Cabinet Meeting and an Exchange With Reporters","publisher":"The American Presidency Project","publication_date":"2022-09-06","url":"https://www.presidency.ucsb.edu/documents/remarks-prior-cabinet-meeting-and-exchange-with-reporters-9","claim":"Biden said the bipartisan infrastructure law, CHIPS Act, Inflation Reduction Act, student debt relief, and American Rescue Plan were his economic agenda and that Cabinet agencies were meeting to lay out implementation plans and spend taxpayer money wisely.","locator":"lines 66-84","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct presidential remarks with explicit implementation instruction and concrete priorities.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"2022","ruler_attribution":"Direct presidential direction to executive agencies.","contrary_evidence":["It remains an internal executive description of intended implementation."],"lenses":["8B.2","8B.3","8B.6","8B.8","8B.10"]}

- Commerce’s CHIPS leadership announcement shows Biden’s administration staffing a program with experienced managers and making accountability and interagency coordination part of execution.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Biden-Harris Administration Announces CHIPS for America Leadership","publisher":"U.S. Department of Commerce","publication_date":"2022-09-20","url":"https://www.commerce.gov/news/press-releases/2022/09/biden-harris-administration-announces-chips-america-leadership","claim":"The administration appointed experienced leaders to run the new CHIPS offices, and Commerce said they would implement the $50 billion semiconductor program with accountability and interagency coordination.","locator":"lines 305-312","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official staffing announcement with named appointees and responsibilities.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"2022","ruler_attribution":"Directly tied to Biden's executive program and appointments.","contrary_evidence":["It shows staffing and intent, not output yet."],"lenses":["8B.2","8B.3","8B.4","8B.5","8B.6","8B.9"]}

- The first CHIPS Implementation Steering Council meeting is strong evidence of whole-of-government coordination, guardrails, and operational follow-through.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Readout of the First Meeting of the CHIPS Implementation Steering Council","publisher":"The American Presidency Project","publication_date":"2022-10-07","url":"https://www.presidency.ucsb.edu/documents/readout-the-first-meeting-the-chips-implementation-steering-council","claim":"The Steering Council met to discuss the CHIPS implementation plan, coordinate across agencies, brief implementation efforts, and set guardrails to protect taxpayer dollars and local communities.","locator":"lines 62-69","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct readout of a formal interagency implementation meeting with named participants and tasks.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"2022","ruler_attribution":"Direct implementation architecture under Biden's executive order.","contrary_evidence":["This is implementation architecture and coordination rather than final outcomes."],"lenses":["8B.2","8B.3","8B.5","8B.6","8B.9","8B.10"]}

- CMS’s IRA fact sheet gives concrete implementation details: the law set Medicare insulin caps, an out-of-pocket cap, lower premiums, and a phased timeline for rollout.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"The Inflation Reduction Act Lowers Health Care Costs for Millions of Americans","publisher":"Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services","publication_date":"2022-10-05","url":"https://www.cms.gov/newsroom/fact-sheets/inflation-reduction-act-lowers-health-care-costs-millions-americans","claim":"CMS said the IRA would cap Medicare insulin at $35 a month, set a $2,000 out-of-pocket drug cap, continue lower Marketplace premiums through 2025, and phase in price negotiation and rebates.","locator":"lines 61-88","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Authoritative implementing-agency fact sheet with concrete policy timelines and benefit design.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"2022","ruler_attribution":"The law and implementation were Biden-sponsored and administered by his agencies.","contrary_evidence":["Many benefits were scheduled for 2023-2025, so 2022 evidence is primarily design and rollout rather than realized savings."],"lenses":["8B.2","8B.6","8B.7","8B.10"]}

- CMS leadership’s signing statement shows administrative readiness to implement the law with stakeholders, states, and firm timelines.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Statements by CMS Leadership on President Biden Signing Inflation Reduction Act into Law","publisher":"Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services","publication_date":"2022-08-16","url":"https://www.cms.gov/newsroom/press-releases/statements-cms-leadership-president-biden-signing-inflation-reduction-act-law","claim":"CMS leadership said it was ready to implement the IRA, would work with stakeholders and states to make the law a reality, and would meet the timelines in the law.","locator":"lines 63-69","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Implementation agency explicitly described readiness, timelines, and coordination with stakeholders.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"2022","ruler_attribution":"Implementation under Biden's executive branch and signed law.","contrary_evidence":["Implementation readiness does not guarantee downstream savings or timely execution."],"lenses":["8B.2","8B.4","8B.5","8B.6","8B.8","8B.9"]}

- Reuters’ July 2022 labor-market report independently corroborates strong output on jobs and pre-pandemic employment recovery, while still reflecting limits on attribution.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"U.S. labor market defies recession fears as job growth surges in July","publisher":"Reuters","publication_date":"2022-08-05","url":"https://www.investing.com/news/economy/us-job-growth-seen-slowing-in-july-but-far-from-recession-levels-2864369","claim":"Reuters reported that U.S. payrolls rose by 528,000 in July 2022, employment moved above its pre-pandemic level, and the labor market remained strong despite recession fears.","locator":"lead and middle paragraphs of the article","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Independent wire reporting on BLS data with clear, datelined figures.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Economy-wide outcome only partly attributable to Biden; reflects Fed, reopening, and inherited conditions as well.","contrary_evidence":["Strong labor data did not remove inflation pressure or prove presidential control over the labor market."],"lenses":["8B.7","8B.9","8B.10"]}

- USDA’s 2022 food-security summary is a clear negative outcome: household food insecurity rose year over year.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Prevalence of U.S. household food insecurity increased in 2022","publisher":"Economic Research Service, U.S. Department of Agriculture","publication_date":"2023-10-25","url":"https://www.ers.usda.gov/data-products/charts-of-note/107694","claim":"USDA said 12.8 percent of U.S. households were food insecure in 2022, up from 10.2 percent in 2021, and very low food security also rose.","locator":"chart note text and summary paragraph","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official annual survey summary with explicit year-over-year comparison and methodology note.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Retrospective evidence about 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Partial and limited; reflects inflation, prices, and post-pandemic stress more than a clean presidential-only effect.","contrary_evidence":["The measure is influenced by inflation and household shocks, so it is not a clean scorecard for presidential control."],"lenses":["8B.7","8B.10"]}

**Search and Rejection Summary**
- I screened roughly 30 documents across the White House archive, Commerce, CMS/HHS, Treasury, DOT, BEA, BLS, Census, USDA, CBO, and Reuters.
- Kept the clearest program-to-implementation-to-outcome chain: Biden’s economic framing, CHIPS staffing and steering, IRA rollout, and independent macro/household outcome data.
- Rejected or did not retain out-of-period 2023-2024 retrospectives, duplicate syndications of the same Reuters stories, and broad rhetorical material that repeated the same “middle out / bottom up” theme without new implementation evidence.
- A direct Commerce implementation-strategy page was search-discovered but blocked on open; I relied instead on the accessible Commerce leadership release and the CHIPS steering-council readout.
- I did not reuse general integrity evidence from 7B because it does not bear directly on effectiveness.

**Remaining Gaps**
- Stronger evidence would be agency audits or award-level records showing realized CHIPS and IRA outputs, not just staffing and rollout architecture.
- Course-correction evidence is thin: I found planning, coordination, and ready-to-implement language, but little direct proof of replacing ineffective implementers or materially reallocating resources in response to failure during 2022.
- Macro outcomes are only partly attributable to Biden. Inflation, real earnings, food insecurity, and household income were shaped heavily by the Fed, global energy and supply shocks, and post-pandemic normalization, so causal attribution remains mixed.
